# Normalize line endings to LF in the working tree on every platform.
#
# This is load-bearing for M9.0's release workflow, not cosmetic. GitHub's windows-latest image
# installs Git for Windows with no `/o:CRLFOption` argument, so the installer default
# `core.autocrlf=true` applies, and actions/checkout never overrides it. Without this file the
# release job checks out ~375 files as CRLF and `pnpm test` reds before packaging is ever reached:
# `tests/unit/agent/skills-contract.test.ts` asserts `skill.startsWith('---\n')` against
# `resources/skills/**/SKILL.md`, which is false for `---\r\n`, and the ```html fence regex stops
# matching too. Measured on a `core.autocrlf=true` clone of this branch: 8 failed / 2664 passed.
#
# `eol=lf` (rather than bare `text=auto`) is what pins the *working tree*, which is what the tests
# read. It also fixes local Windows clones and any future Windows job, instead of papering over the
# one assertion that happens to notice today.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures whose bytes are asserted on must never be touched by any filter, even if a future
# heuristic misjudges them as text. M4.6's PPTX round-trip identity gate compares archives
# byte-for-byte; a line-ending rewrite there would be a silent, confusing failure.
*.pptx  binary
*.potx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.gif   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.asar  binary
*.exe   binary
